---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { skills, credentials } from '../data/site';
---
<Layout title="About — Javier Ponz" description="Background, experience, skills and credentials of Javier Ponz: ten years of technical service at Apple Retail, now building AI systems and full stack applications.">
<section class="hero"><p class="kicker">About</p><h1>Leading is <span class="serif">serving</span>.</h1><p class="lede">Everything I do professionally comes back to that. Diagnose what is actually wrong, understand who it affects, say it in words that don't humiliate anyone, and leave the person with more control than they had before.</p></section>

<section class="grid"><span class="label">The principle</span><div class="prose">
<p>I mean it in three directions, and they are the same skill.</p>
<p><strong>Leading yourself.</strong> Doing the unglamorous part without an audience — the tests, the release gate, the honest README, the second look at the thing that already works. Nobody claps for it, and it is what separates finished from nearly finished.</p>
<p><strong>Leading others one at a time.</strong> Mentoring and teaching, which is mostly restraint: giving someone the reasoning instead of the answer, and being willing to be slower now so they are faster later.</p>
<p><strong>Leading teams.</strong> Removing what is in their way. As a lead the job was never to be the most capable person in the room; it was to make the room work — resources, cover, conflict handled early, credit pointed at whoever earned it.</p>
<p>Service is not the soft edge of technical work. It is the part that decides whether the technical work reaches anyone.</p>
</div></section>

<section class="grid"><span class="label">Experience</span><div class="copy"><h2>Apple Retail · 2014—2024</h2><p>Ten years and five roles: Specialist, Technical Specialist, AppleCare Advisor, Genius, then In-Store Experience Lead across Parquesur and Xanadú in Madrid. The through line was diagnosis under pressure — find the real fault fast, explain it in terms the person in front of you actually cares about, and keep their trust while doing it.</p><p>Thousands of people arrived angry, confused, or holding a dead machine with their only copy of something on it. That is where I learned that the technical answer and the useful answer are not always the same sentence.</p><p>As a lead I ran operations across two stores — opening and closing, events, resourcing, maintenance, and supporting teams when a situation did not fit any procedure. Leading there meant clearing obstacles, not issuing instructions.</p></div></section>

<section class="grid"><span class="label">Now</span><div class="copy"><h2>AI systems and full stack</h2><p>Since moving to Aberdeen I have been building AI systems full time: agent workflows with Hermes Agent and OpenClaw, MCP servers, automations in Docker, and applications deployed on my own VPS with Traefik and TLS. I am strongest where systems fail — investigating failure modes, debugging workflows, turning unpredictable behaviour into something documented and reliable.</p><p>It is the same job as before, aimed at a different surface. A person stuck on a machine and a machine stuck on a task both need someone willing to find the real fault and write down what it was. <a href="/thesis">My thesis</a> is that the tools worth building are the ones that hand control back to the person using them.</p><p>I am early in this career and deliberately public about it: every project has source code, tests and a stated scope. I would rather be judged on what I have shipped than on how I describe myself.</p></div></section>

<section class="grid"><span class="label">Skills</span><div class="copy">{Object.entries(skills).map(([group, items]) => <div class="skillgroup"><h3>{group}</h3><div class="tags">{items.map((item) => <span class="tag">{item}</span>)}</div></div>)}</div></section>

<section class="grid"><span class="label">Credentials</span><div class="rows">{credentials.map((c) => <article class="row"><time>{c.date}</time><div><h3>{c.title}</h3><p>{c.org}{c.org && c.status ? ' · ' : ''}{c.status}</p></div></article>)}</div></section>

<section class="grid"><span class="label">In short</span><div class="notice">Good technical work leaves people with more clarity and more control than they had before. That is the whole standard.</div></section>
</Layout>
